"The words “classical music” can mean all of the Western art music tradition, from the Middle Ages to today. But there is also a narrower Classical period. In this lesson we look at three periods that are often named: the Baroque, roughly 1600 to 1750, the Classical, roughly 1750 to 1820, and the Romantic, roughly the nineteenth century. The dates are only approximate, and the books disagree about them. Much Baroque music has a basso continuo: a bass line that keeps going, played by a bass instrument together with an instrument that can play chords, such as a harpsichord or an organ. Performers often added ornaments, such as trills and mordents. A harpsichord cannot change its loudness by how hard you press the key, so the dynamics were often terraced, a step from piano to forte. But not all of it: later composers, such as Vivaldi, used more varied dynamics. Classical music tends to have a lighter, clearer texture. It is mainly homophonic, with a clear melody over a chordal accompaniment. The phrases are balanced and brief, typically four bars long. A common accompaniment is the Alberti bass, which plays the notes of a chord in the order lowest, highest, middle, highest. The symphony and the string quartet became prominent, and the piano gradually replaced the harpsichord. Romantic composers sought to make music that was individual, emotional, and dramatic. They used a wider range of dynamics, for example from ppp to fff, and more chromatic harmony. The orchestra grew, with as many as 120 players for the works of Wagner and Mahler. The modern piano, with its more powerful and more sustained tone, took over from the fortepiano. And the inspiration was not only love: it could be nature, literature, or poetry. The periods overlap, and many composers do not fit just one. Bach (1685–1750) is a late Baroque composer. Mozart (1756–1791) is a Classical composer. Chopin (1810–1849) is an early Romantic composer. Beethoven (1770–1827) was mentored during the Classical period, and his style was a key driver of the transition to Romantic music. Schubert (1797–1828) belongs to the late Classical and early Romantic eras. The tune on this page is our own, and the three versions are only teaching imitations of some typical features. Real composers varied a great deal. One musicologist points out that there is too much diversity within the music of the time to make sweeping generalizations. So treat the names as helpful handles, and listen to real music with open ears.": "c19832780448",
